    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Numerous studies have found that obese workers are paid lower wages than the nonobese, even when controlling for skill level and productivity. Bhattacharya and Bundorf (2009) find that in the United States, this wage differential is only significant in jobs that provide health insurance to their employees and that this wage differential largely results from the increased cost of insuring obese workers. I examine European data from the Survey on Health Retirement and Aging in Europe (SHARE) to determine whether obese workers are paid lower wages than the non-obese, even where the majority of health insurance is provided by the state. I find that there is no significant wage differential between the obese and non-obese in Europe as a whole and that to the extent that wage differentials exist in individual countries, these are highly correlated with the prevalence of employer-provided health insurance in those countries.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
